## The split

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {tickets} {state} />

## What each problem is

### `parser`: the parser reads day-first dates month first · fixed

The shared parser read every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so a
date with both parts at twelve or less came out flipped: "7 April" went out as "4 July". This is
the bug #4471 named, fixed by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. It reached tenants through
the app, by email, and through booking-v2's form, whose hint (DD/MM/YYYY) matches how they write.

### `hint`: booking-v3's form told people to type the month first · won't fix

booking-v3 printed MM/DD/YYYY under its date field. People did what the hint said and typed the
month first; the form read the date the tenant's way, day first, and flipped it. Only reports that
came in through the form, at tenants on booking-v3, are this problem. The parser fix did not touch
it. It is won't fix because booking-v3 is retired: with the form gone, nothing prints that hint.

### `one-day`: the date moves one day earlier · open

Not a flip. The date lands one day early, and only sometimes: "Tuesday 6 October showed as
Monday", one meeting reached Lisbon as Tuesday and Toronto as Monday, an invite arrived before the
booking was made. That points at a date with no time being turned into a moment and shown in
another time zone. The desk already read these reports as a one-day move
([desk notes](https://github.com/MateuszKruk1303/support-kb-4471/blob/main/src/lib/data/desk-notes.json)),
filed them under `parser` by tenant and footnoted them. A day-first parser does not move a date by a
day, so the parser fix leaves them as they are. Next step: check how booking dates cross time zones
between the booking and the invite.

## How the split is made

- **One id, one report.** The ticket export repeats some rows; each report counts once.
- **The tenant decides the cause, not intake.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser;
  the cause comes from the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`). The "tagged at intake as"
  column shows how far the two disagree. Do not count by tag.
- **Counted the way the desk counts.** Every report stays where the desk's own table files it, by
  tenant. A report in the desk's one-day-move notes is counted as a one-day move as well, so those
  reports sit in two rows (`problems.ts`).
- **Every number here is built from the data at render time.** The states come from the
  engineering update that closed #4471; the counts come from the export.

